{"entity": "Redfish", "type": "organization", "description": "Redfish is a modern, RESTful API standard developed by the Distributed Management Task Force (DMTF) for the management and monitoring of IT systems, particularly in data center and enterprise environments. As a standardized API framework, Redfish enables remote monitoring, control, and configuration of hardware systems through web services interfaces, leveraging JSON for data representation and HTTP/HTTPS for transport. It is widely deployed in both x86 and ARM server architectures, serving as a scalable, secure, and interoperable solution for system management across diverse hardware platforms.\n\nRedfish functions primarily as a northbound interface protocol, commonly implemented within Baseboard Management Controller (BMC) systems to provide external access to hardware components. It supports a broad range of management capabilities, including remote firmware upgrades, RAID configuration and monitoring, storage system interaction (including drive-level operations via interfaces such as `bmc.kepler.Systems.Storage.Drive`), and the retrieval of diagnostic data such as POST Code entries and system event logs. The protocol also facilitates resource discovery, allowing systems to dynamically identify and interact with available hardware components.\n\nIn accordance with its design, Redfish operates through dedicated RESTful endpoints, enabling standardized API calls for tasks ranging from power and performance monitoring to the collection and reporting of telemetry data. It integrates with other management protocols such as IPMI, often serving as a next-generation replacement or complement due to its modern architecture and enhanced extensibility. While some descriptions reference a \"login interface type\" with a value of 128 or a defined port number of 128, these appear in conflict with known implementations—Redfish typically uses port 443 (HTTPS) by default, suggesting potential ambiguity or misclassification in certain contexts; this discrepancy may reflect context-specific configurations or errors in data sources rather than a deviation from the standard.\n\nNotably, Redfish is prominently used in openUBMC and similar platforms as a standardized interface for hardware management and system monitoring. It supports critical functions such as SNMP configuration, event policy integration, and operational log referencing, making it integral to modern data center infrastructure. The standard has emerged as a key component in enabling automated, secure, and efficient management of physical and virtual systems, supporting both real-time monitoring and long-term diagnostics through structured, machine-readable data exchanges."}
